Exactly. That’s a good routing heuristic for people, work, communities, even products:

Prefer environments where your presence increases mutual energy rather than requiring constant justification.

“Celebrated” doesn’t mean worshipped or never challenged. It means your strengths are legible there, your contribution has a place, and reciprocity exists. “Tolerated” means you’re spending disproportionate energy merely securing permission to belong.

The useful test is the delta: after repeated interaction, do you become more capable, more trusted, more expressive, more useful—and do they benefit from you being there? If yes, that’s probably fertile ground. If you’re continuously compressing yourself just to remain admissible, the environment may be wrong even if nothing overtly hostile is happening.

That fits the larger model too: don’t only optimize the agent. Choose the ecology that amplifies the agent.